# Code Review & Quality Gates

Apply these quality gates when preparing commits, self-reviewing changes, or reviewing pull requests, alongside the standards in `.cursor/rules/core/` and `.cursor/rules/quality/`.

---

## 1. Review Scope & Severity Classification

- **Diff-Scoped Review**: Apply review gates strictly to the files and lines introduced or modified by the PR—do not require refactoring untouched legacy code outside the diff.
- **Blocking Gates (Must Pass Before Merge)**: Any violation that causes build/test failures, security or authorization flaws, data or financial corruption, transaction or concurrency defects, unhandled resource/memory leaks, or breaks core language safety rules (`eval`, `debugger`, `var`, shadowed/undeclared variables, loose/reference wrapper equality, missing `{}` braces or `switch` termination, missing `@Override` or paired `equals`/`hashCode`, field injection, circular dependencies, constructor I/O, or empty `catch` blocks).
- **Standard Gates (Fix or Justify in PR Comment)**: Conventions, structural patterns, query/performance optimizations, and maintainability expectations (naming suffixes, member ordering, `const` preference, optional chaining, explicit contract types, guard clauses, thin controllers, HTTP status semantics, read-only/bounded queries, N+1 avoidance, entry-point logging, and code formatting).

---

## 2. Build, Static Analysis & Dependency Gates

- **Blocking**:
  - **Clean Build & CI Suite**: All modified modules must compile cleanly and pass existing CI test suites with zero errors.
  - **Zero Linter, Type & Static Analysis Blockers**: Changed files must introduce zero linter errors, compiler/type-checker errors, or Blocker/Critical static analysis issues.
  - **Lockfile Synchronization**: Whenever a dependency manifest changes, the corresponding lockfile must be updated in the same commit.
  - **No Unused or Unstable Dependencies**: Remove unused dependencies from manifests and never introduce unstable or prerelease (`SNAPSHOT`) versions in production builds.
  - **Shared Module Versioning**: Bump the version of any modified shared internal library/module and update consumer references.
- **Standard**:
  - **Version Pinning & Non-Deprecated APIs**: Pin related library families using shared version properties, and do not call deprecated APIs in new code.
  - **Documented Build & Config Impact**: Justify any new Major static analysis warning, added/removed dependency, new externalized configuration property, or significant bundle/artifact size increase (>50 KB gzipped) in the PR description.

---

## 3. File, Code & Commit Hygiene Gates (Standard)

- **Feature & Layer Encapsulation**: Place new files inside their corresponding feature, shared, or layer folder—never leave loose files at the application root, and do not introduce conflicting sibling route paths.
- **File Formatting & Cleanliness**:
  - Use UTF-8 encoding, indent with spaces only (no tabs), and end every file with a single newline.
  - Maintain consistent spacing around keywords and operators without manual multi-space column alignment, and keep lines within the project's configured line-length limit.
  - Remove trailing whitespace, unused imports, and dead code before committing.
- **No Unrelated Churn**: Keep each PR scoped to a single ticket/concern and do not reformat untouched files or mix unrelated refactors.
- **Comments & Tracked TODOs**:
  - Document public APIs on shared modules and keep comments synchronized when behavior changes.
  - Include a tracking ticket ID on every `TODO` or `FIXME` comment.
  - Delete commented-out code unless accompanied by a comment above it with a tracking ticket ID and removal condition.
- **Commit & PR Format**:
  - Prefix every commit message and PR title with the ticket ID (e.g., `TICKET-123: concise summary`).
  - Include the ticket heading, ticket link, summary of changes, verification notes, and any dependency or configuration notes in the PR description.
